{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n17 \n \n \narrays could be designed to support future experiments, but they cannot extend downstream of \nthe burner tip without interfering with other (e.g., optical) measurements. \n \nThe two lone thermocouples, which are both at nominally 200 mm from the center of the spherical \nburner, were installed throughout ACME testing.  The tip of one was seen to be slightly bent early \nin testing, as discussed and depicted in section 3.G of the ACME Engineering Implementation \nreport.   \n \nThe short (or far) version of the thermocouple rake was used throughout ACME testing, except for \nthe final day of CFI-G testing and first round-two day of Flame Design testing, which both featured \nnormal flames, even though second rounds of each study were otherwise with inverse flames.   \nFactors which led to the limited use of the short thermocouple rake included the need for crew \ntime to switch between rakes and the potential adverse effects, such as heat loss and quenching, \nof the long (close) rake.  The short (far) rake\u2019s thermocouple tips should nominally be at 45, 60, \n100, and 100 mm from the spherical burner center.  Meanwhile, the thermocouples of the long \n(near) rake should nominally be at 13, 25, 50, and 100 mm from the spherical burner center.  \n \nFor thermocouples at the same distance from the burner center, the angle between the rays from \nthe burner center to the two thermocouples should be 90 degrees.  The bead size of the \nthermocouples was not measured, but it is undoubtedly at least twice the size of the 0.2 mm \ndiameter wire. \n  \nThe two thermocouples closest to the burner outlets on each four-thermocouple rake are type B, \nwhile the others are type K. \n \nExperiment-unique hardware is also mounted on the chamber insert.  For example, to \naccommodate the E-FIELD Flames experiment, the insert is equipped with a high-voltage supply \nand a crew-removable electrode mesh which can be set between +10 and -10 kV.   The circular mesh \nis orthogonal to the chamber axis and is positioned (during set up) at either ~35 or ~50 mm \ndownstream of the insulated burner.  The ion current between the burner and electrode mesh will \nbe determined via measurement of the voltage drop across a known resistor and application of \nOhm\u2019s Law. \n \nAdditional images of the chamber insert can be seen in the ACME Engineering Implementation", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 17}}